From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server, win32u, user32, winex11: the foreground lock, as Windows' SetForegroundWindow rules

Wine had no foreground lock: any program could take the foreground and the
keyboard -- one started in the background (over ssh, a service's helper, a
slow program whose window came after the person moved on) took the keys from
the window being typed into (Setup, i0638).

As on Windows, a program's SetForegroundWindow (and an activating ShowWindow
or SetWindowPos of its window) brings the window forward only when there is no
foreground window, the program is the foreground process or was started by it
(or by a program it started), it or a program that started it got the person's
last input (a key or button press, a hotkey, a key a low-level hook took; the
Windows key alone is the shell's), it holds an AllowSetForegroundWindow grant
(or ASFW_ANY), it or the foreground process is being debugged, or the person's
last input is older than ForegroundLockTimeout (200000 ms, Windows' default; 0
turns the lock off) -- and never while another program's menu is open or
another program holds LockSetForegroundWindow (lifted by Alt and by input that
changes the foreground). Grants end when the person's input goes to another
program. The drivers' foreground changes (the window manager's, the
compositor's: the person's own click) are not held
(NtUserSetForegroundWindowInput).

A window kept back is not activated (a click on it activates it, also when its
program set the focus to it before showing it), goes right under the window in
front (also in X, and an exposure does not raise it over it; the lock's timer
counts keys, buttons and the wheel, not the pointer moves X makes when a
window maps under it), and its taskbar button flashes
(FLASHW_TRAY|FLASHW_TIMERNOFG, ForegroundFlashCount); after the last flash the
button stays lit until the window is activated. Only a program that may set
the foreground changes ForegroundLockTimeout. Accounts made before the lock
have Wine's old 0 there: it becomes 200000 once.

The right goes with what such a program starts, whatever the launch chain: a
program it starts gets a grant of its own at creation (it holds once the
starter has exited), and a Unix program it starts (a bridge that starts the
real window again, as Setup's does) is handed a token in its environment
(WINESGFGTOKEN) that the Wine programs that one starts claim; tokens are
issued only for the starter's own right, end when the person's input goes
elsewhere, and are claimed once.
